{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781331244,"nonce":"spoB/0p6zh0xmKQVwxv8ZtOfG20=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABFW8oTQ","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/15","origin":"github"},"title":"Create launches requirements.Values[0], not the cost-optimal instance type","message":"When a NodePool permits multiple instance types, Karpenter passes the full compatible set in the NodeClaim and expects the cloudprovider to launch the best (cheapest available) one. This provider takes the first value verbatim.\n\n### Evidence\n- `pkg/cloudprovider/cloudprovider.go:227` `pickInstanceType` → `requirementValue(nc, corev1.LabelInstanceTypeStable)`.\n- `pkg/cloudprovider/cloudprovider.go:276-283` `requirementValue` returns `r.Values[0]` — the lexically-first value, not price-sorted.\n\n### Impact\nIf a NodePool allows N instance types, the provider always launches the first by name regardless of spot/on-demand price, forgoing Karpenter's core cost-optimization. Latent today for users who pin exactly one `node.kubernetes.io/instance-type` (e.g. #11), but wrong for the common multi-type case.\n\n### Suggested fix\n- Among the instance types passed to `Create` (intersected with the NodeClaim requirements), select by price using the offerings already computed in `translate()` (spot market price for spot, on-demand price otherwise), honoring availability.\n- Return the launched type (already handled by `hydrateClaim`).\n- Test: a NodeClaim allowing 3 types with distinct prices launches the cheapest available.","files":null}]}